  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 422 AM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nHurricane Isaias made landfall near Destin yesterday evening and has \nweakened to a Post-Tropical Cyclone as it tracks northward. While \nthe system continues to pull east, strong wind gust will remain the \nprimary concern today across much of the area. High-resolution \nguidance show strong consensus for up to 40-50 mph gusts in the \nGolden Triangle down to central MS by mid-morning, with 30+ mph \ngusts possible across the rest of our area by mid-day. While the \nTropical Storm Warning has been canceled a Wind Advisory has been \nadded to those areas. Most of the forecast area will be under an \nWind Advisory, with only the counties of western ARK and western LA \nparishes excluded. Downed trees and power lines are possible, \nespecially in the east and northeast MS where the highest gusts are \nanticipated over the longest duration.\n\nRainfall coverage will drop off sharply from the northeast to the \nsouthwest, where the heaviest rainfall corridor being confined to \nthe far northeast thus the flood watch remains in effect through \nthis evening. 1-3 inches over a short period of time is expected. \nFarther south and west, rainfall drops off significantly, with parts \nof southeast ARK, northeast LA, and southwest MS likely to see \nlittle to no rain. All rain will taper of by this evening.\n\nThick cloud cover and ongoing rainfall across the northeast will \ncreate a sharp gradient in temperatures from northeast to southwest \nwith portions of the region struggling to reach the lower 70s and \nothers reaching the lower 90s.\n\nDry conditions are expected to rapidly return on Sunday as the \nsystem leaves the region. Lighter winds will prevent fireWx danger \non Sunday, but rapidly dropping RH values of under 40% and high \nwinds will lead to increased fireWx danger across our southeast \ntoday. /KP/",
